                    "#Clamp a copper wire to one end of the batteries. Usually, you'll have a wire with red clips and one with black. Hook one end of the red clips up to the positive end of the batteries, but leave the other clip off for now. If you hook everything up, you'll actually be turning the light bulb on before you're ready. You could get burned if you're not careful<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 5.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 5-preview Version 5.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 8.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Stand up the two remaining clips, then clamp the graphite between them. Think of making an H-shape, where the two clips are the sides and the graphite is the horizontal line in the middle.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 5.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 6-preview Version 5.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 8.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Put the glass jar on top of the clips and graphite. This isn't strictly necessary, as the graphite would still light even without the jar. But the process creates smoke, and the graphite can shatter. Moreover, having an actual \"bulb\" helps create a more even light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 5.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 7-preview Version 5.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 8.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the final wire to the other end of the batteries to turn the light on. You are making a simple circuit, connecting the batteries in an electrical loop. The light comes from the graphite -- as electricity flows through it, it heats up and emits energy in the form of light and heat. Thus, your light bulb![[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 8 Version 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Troubleshoot to get a stronger light. If the light is weak, or isn't working, there are some things you can do to make sure you get a working light.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 9 Version 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut some copper wire into two sections, roughly 1-2 feet each. Copper is a great conductor of electricity, and most wiring is copper. You need two strands about 1.5 feet (0.4572 m) long.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 10 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove 1\" of shielding from each end of the wire. The shielding is the rubber that covers the copper wire. Removing it can be achieved by carefully squeezing the cable cutter so that it cuts through the shielding but not the wire. The shielding can them be stripped from the ends with your hands.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 11 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 11 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use the nail to pierce two holes into a cork. The holes should be in the middle of the cork about 1/2\" apart. This cork will hold your wires in place. Think of it as the bottom, metal part of your light bulb.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 12 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Thread one end of each copper wire through the cork. Leave about 2\" or so exposed above the top of the cork.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 13 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 13-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 13 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 13 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 13 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Bend the end of the two strands inside the cork into a hook shape. The hook of each strand should curve the same way. This will hold onto your filament, the part of the bulb that actually lights up.",
                    "#Cut the iron wire into 1-1/2 to 2 inch (3.8cm to 5cm) strips. Make five strips. The thinner your wire is before beginning, the better. Thinner wire will almost always result in a better light.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Twist the five iron wire lengths together with your fingers. You want a long braid of wire. It needs to be tightly wound as well.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 16 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 16-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 16 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 16 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 16 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place the twisted iron wires onto the copper hooks, making sure it touches both copper strands. For an even better fit, you can tighten the hooks around your iron filament. The more of it touching, the better.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 17 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 17-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 17 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 17 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 17 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Lay the jar on top of the cork. This is your \"bulb.\" It will help protect from shock, but also creates a more concentrated light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 18 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 18-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 18 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 18 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 18 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Wrap both ends of the wire around separate ends of a battery to turn the light on. If you have electrical clamps, they will be much safer and easier to use. If you don't, make sure you're wearing rubber-soled shoes and have safety gloves on. Attach one wire to each end of the battery to complete the circuit and \"turn on\" the light bulb.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 19 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 19-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 19 Version 3.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 19 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 19 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Troubleshoot if you're not getting enough light. This is a simple scientific machine, so their are simple ways to test and fix bulbs that don't work.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 20 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Head to your local art store and pick up some pencil lead. You should use the kind you get in mechanical pencils -- the thin, solo pieces of graphite that you can usually buy in packs. The thinner, the better -- try something like .5mm thickness.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Round up the supplies needed for you bulb. Everything here is easy to round up at a local hardware store, if you don't already have it all at home. You'll need.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 2 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Hook the copper wires up to the electrical clips. The ends of each wire should have a clip on it. If you don't have clips, you can still make a light-bulb. To do so, curl each end of the wire into a little round hook.<span id=\"series\"></span><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 3 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 3-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 3 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 3 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 3 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach your batteries together in series. This simply means you tape them together, end to end, so that they all work together to provide power. Make sure you line them up positive end to negative end. Then use electrical tape to secure all the batteries together into a long rod.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 4 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 4-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 4 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 4 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 4 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Clamp a copper wire to one end of the batteries. Usually, you'll have a wire with red clips and one with black. Hook one end of the red clips up to the positive end of the batteries, but leave the other clip off for now. If you hook everything up, you'll actually be turning the light bulb on before you're ready. You could get burned if you're not careful<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 5-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 5 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Stand up the two remaining clips, then clamp the graphite between them. Think of making an H-shape, where the two clips are the sides and the graphite is the horizontal line in the middle.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 6-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 6 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Put the glass jar on top of the clips and graphite. This isn't strictly necessary, as the graphite would still light even without the jar. But the process creates smoke, and the graphite can shatter. Moreover, having an actual \"bulb\" helps create a more even light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 7-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 7 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Attach the final wire to the other end of the batteries to turn the light on. You are making a simple circuit, connecting the batteries in an electrical loop. The light comes from the graphite -- as electricity flows through it, it heats up and emits energy in the form of light and heat. Thus, your light bulb!<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 8 Version 4.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 8-preview Version 4.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 8 Version 7.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 8 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 8 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Troubleshoot to get a stronger light. If the light is weak, or isn't working, there are some things you can do to make sure you get a working light.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 9 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut some copper wire into two sections, roughly 1-2 feet each. Copper is a great conductor of electricity, and most wiring is copper. You need two strands about 1.5 feet (0.4572 m) long.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 10.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 10-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 10 Version 4.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove 1\" of shielding from each end of the wire. The shielding is the rubber that covers the copper wire. Removing it can be achieved by carefully squeezing the cable cutter so that it cuts through the shielding but not the wire. The shielding can them be stripped from the ends with your hands.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 11.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 11-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 11 Version 4.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 11.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 11.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Use the nail to pierce two holes into a cork. The holes should be in the middle of the cork about 1/2\" apart. This cork will hold your wires in place. Think of it as the bottom, metal part of your light bulb.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 12.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 12-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 12 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Thread one end of each copper wire through the cork. Leave about 2\" or so exposed above the top of the cork.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 13.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 13-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 13 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 13.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 13.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Bend the end of the two strands inside the cork into a hook shape. The hook of each strand should curve the same way. This will hold onto your filament, the part of the bulb that actually lights up.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut the iron wire into 1-1/2 to 2 inch (3.8cm to 5cm) strips. Make five strips. The thinner your wire is before beginning, the better. Thinner wire will almost always result in a better light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 15.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 15-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 15 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 15.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 15.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Twist the five iron wire lengths together with your fingers. You want a long braid of wire. It needs to be tightly wound as well.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 16.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 16-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 16 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 16.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 16.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place the twisted iron wires onto the copper hooks, making sure it touches both copper strands. For an even better fit, you can tighten the hooks around your iron filament. The more of it touching, the better.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 17.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 17-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 17 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 17.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 17.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Lay the jar on top of the cork. This is your \"bulb.\" It will help protect from shock, but also creates a more concentrated light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 18.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 18-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 18 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 18.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 18.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Wrap both ends of the wire around separate ends of a battery to turn the light on. If you have electrical clamps, they will be much safer and easier to use. If you don't, make sure you're wearing rubber-soled shoes and have safety gloves on. Attach one wire to each end of the battery to complete the circuit and \"turn on\" the light bulb.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Light Bulb Step 19.360p.mp4|Make a Light Bulb Step 19-preview.jpg|Make a Light Bulb Step 19 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Light Bulb Step 19.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Light Bulb Step 19.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Troubleshoot if you're not getting enough light. This is a simple scientific machine, so their are simple ways to test and fix bulbs that don't work.[[Image:Make a Light Bulb Step 20 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
